# Sugya Source Interpretation Analysis Prompt v1

You are an expert in the structure of the Babylonian Talmud, Rabbinic hermeneutics, and the interpretation of Tanakh within the Gemara.

You are given FOUR files.

FILE 1

berakhot_bundle.jsonl

This is the original source text.

This is the PRIMARY source.

All conclusions must ultimately be based on this file.

---

FILE 2

berakhot_sugya_analysis_pass1.json

This defines the sugya boundaries.

Do NOT redefine the sugyot.

Use these boundaries exactly.

---

FILE 3

berakhot_authority_analysis_all_sugyot.json

This identifies the authorities and sources.

Use this only as a helper.

Always verify against the original bundle.

---

FILE 4

berakhot_argument_analysis_all_sugyot_v3_expanded_details.json

This identifies the arguments.

Use it only as a guide.

Always verify conclusions against the original bundle.

====================================================

PRIMARY OBJECTIVE

====================================================

Analyze every sugya independently.

Your purpose is NOT to summarize the sugya.

Your purpose is to reconstruct HOW the Gemara interprets every source that appears in the sugya.

Specifically determine:

• why each source is introduced

• how each source is interpreted

• which rabbi interprets it

• how different rabbis interpret the same source differently

• what textual features of the source drive the interpretation

• how each interpretation advances the argument

• how each interpretation relates to the overall purpose of the sugya

The final output must be ONE valid JSON document.

====================================================

MULTI-PASS ANALYSIS

====================================================

Perform this analysis in six passes.

PASS 1

Locate every source.

Include:

Torah

Nevi'im

Ketuvim

Mishnah

Tosefta

Baraita

Amoraic statements

Named rabbis

Narrative precedents

Logical principles

Earlier discussions referenced by the Gemara

PASS 2

For every source determine

why it appears.

Classify its function.

Choose one or more:

FOUNDATION

PROOF

TEXTUAL PROOF

QUESTION

CHALLENGE

OBJECTION

DISTINCTION

LIMITATION

RESOLUTION

APPLICATION

ILLUSTRATION

NARRATIVE SUPPORT

THEOLOGICAL SUPPORT

ETHICAL SUPPORT

PASS 3

Analyze the text itself.

Determine

exactly

which words

phrases

or grammatical constructions

are significant.

Especially for Tanakh,

identify

specific Hebrew words

that the Gemara builds its interpretation upon.

Do NOT merely summarize the verse.

Explain WHY those words matter.

PASS 4

Determine how every rabbinic participant understands the source.

For every participant identify

whether the source is read

literally

contextually

grammatically

midrashically

conceptually

analogically

through a gezerah shavah

through a kal vachomer

through another hermeneutic principle

or through practical halakhic reasoning.

PASS 5

Compare all interpretations.

Determine

where rabbis agree

where they disagree

whether they interpret

the same Hebrew words differently

different Hebrew words

or the same words with different assumptions.

Determine whether later participants

expand

limit

qualify

reinterpret

harmonize

or reject

earlier interpretations.

PASS 6

Generate the final JSON.

